Subject: [PATCH] i386: add the elfsysvnt target

The triple's os and abi fields are honestly linux-gnu, so config.gcc already
routes x86_64-elfsysvnt-linux-gnu through the ordinary x86_64 Linux arm and
the compiler builds without this. What it does not do without this is carry
the two things the target mandates rather than suggests, and both are values
some package can forget to pass.

-mno-red-zone, by TARGET_SUBTARGET_DEFAULT rather than by a spec string.
Spike 3 measured Cygwin's signal delivery taking the word at %rsp-8 on every
delivery where the host itself takes nothing, so one object compiled with a
red zone corrupts a stack at an unpredictable later date in a package nobody
was looking at. -mred-zone still turns it back on, deliberately: WP-43 may
retire the flag if a delivery that reserves the 128 bytes prices out cheaply,
and a target that refused the option outright would have to be rebuilt to
find out.

__ELFSYSVNT__, because config.guess cannot read the vendor field off uname
here -- sysname is Linux by design, so that the configure scripts branching
on it keep working. It asks the compiler instead, the way it already asks
which libc it is looking at. Without this define a native build silently
configures as x86_64-pc-linux-gnu, which is the failure DR-0001 chose the
vendor field to avoid.

Nothing else is touched. GLIBC_DYNAMIC_LINKER64 in i386/linux64.h is already
/lib64/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2, which is what doc/target-definition.md fixes the
loader SONAME at, so the two agree without a patch.

Against gcc 13.3.0, pinned in ../gcc.pin.

--- a/gcc/config.gcc
+++ b/gcc/config.gcc
@@ -1980,6 +1980,14 @@
 	x86_64-*-linux*)
 		tm_file="${tm_file} linux.h linux-android.h i386/linux-common.h i386/linux64.h"
 		extra_options="${extra_options} linux-android.opt"
+		# The vendor field is what distinguishes this target, since its os
+		# and abi fields are honestly linux-gnu and everything above reads
+		# them.  DR-0001 records why the name sits there.
+		case ${target} in
+		x86_64-elfsysvnt-*)
+			tm_file="${tm_file} i386/elfsysvnt.h"
+			;;
+		esac
 	  	;;
 	x86_64-*-kfreebsd*-gnu)
 		tm_file="${tm_file} kfreebsd-gnu.h i386/kfreebsd-gnu64.h"
--- /dev/null
+++ b/gcc/config/i386/elfsysvnt.h
@@ -0,0 +1,51 @@
+/* Target definitions for x86-64 ELF and System V on Windows NT, over Cygwin.
+   Copyright (C) 2026 Philip Dye.
+
+This file is part of GCC.
+
+GCC is free software; you can redistribute it and/or modify it under the terms
+of the GNU General Public License as published by the Free Software Foundation;
+either version 3, or (at your option) any later version.
+
+GCC is distributed in the hope that it will be useful, but WITHOUT ANY
+WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A
+PARTICULAR PURPOSE.  See the GNU General Public License for more details.
+
+You should have received a copy of the GNU General Public License along with
+GCC; see the file COPYING3.  If not see <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.
+
+This header carries what the target mandates rather than what a caller
+remembers.  Everything in it is a value some package can forget to pass, which
+is the definition of a thing that belongs here instead of in a makefile.  The
+target definition it answers to is doc/target-definition.md.  */
+
+/* The red zone is not available on this platform, and the reason is ours
+   rather than Windows'.  Spike 3 measured the host leaving the reserved 128
+   bytes alone under preemption, thread hijacking and its own exception
+   dispatch, and measured Cygwin's signal delivery taking the word at %rsp-8
+   on every single delivery.  One object compiled with a red zone corrupts a
+   stack at an unpredictable later date, in a package nobody was looking at,
+   which is close to the worst shape a defect can take.
+
+   So it is defaulted here rather than passed.  -mred-zone still turns it back
+   on: WP-43 may retire this flag entirely if a delivery that reserves the 128
+   bytes proves cheap enough, and a target that refused the option outright
+   would have to be rebuilt to find out.  Hand-written assembly is not reached
+   by this at all, which is why WP-16 delivers a ledger of it.  */
+#undef  TARGET_SUBTARGET_DEFAULT
+#define TARGET_SUBTARGET_DEFAULT MASK_NO_RED_ZONE
+
+/* config.guess cannot read the vendor field off uname here: sysname is Linux
+   by design, so that the thousands of configure scripts branching on it keep
+   working.  It asks the compiler instead, the way it already asks which libc
+   it is looking at, and this is the answer it asks for.  Removing this define
+   makes a native build silently configure as x86_64-pc-linux-gnu.  */
+#undef  TARGET_OS_CPP_BUILTINS
+#define TARGET_OS_CPP_BUILTINS()		\
+  do						\
+    {						\
+      GNU_USER_TARGET_OS_CPP_BUILTINS ();	\
+      ANDROID_TARGET_OS_CPP_BUILTINS ();	\
+      builtin_define ("__ELFSYSVNT__");		\
+    }						\
+  while (0)
